## Thread pools

Whereas a single `Thread` runs one routine, a thread pool can run many jobs in parallel on a fixed set of worker threads. Use a thread pool when you have a large number of independent tasks that should run concurrently without spawning an unbounded number of threads.

### Creating a pool

Import the `stdlib/os/thread-pool` module and create a `ThreadPool`. By default the pool sizes itself to the number of CPU cores. You can pass an explicit worker thread count instead:

```spice
import "std/os/thread-pool";

f<int> main() {
    // One worker thread per CPU core
    ThreadPool pool = ThreadPool();

    // Or a fixed number of worker threads
    ThreadPool pool2 = ThreadPool(4s);

    // Do something with the pools
}
```

### Enqueueing and running jobs

The pool has to be started before it accepts jobs. Use `enqueue()` to add a job and `start()` to launch the worker threads. `start()` is idempotent, so calling it multiple times is safe:

```spice
import "std/os/thread-pool";

f<int> main() {
    ThreadPool pool = ThreadPool(4s);

    pool.enqueue(p() {
        printf("Job A\n");
    });
    pool.enqueue(p() {
        printf("Job B\n");
    });

    // Start the worker threads
    pool.start();
}
```

### Waiting for the pool to finish

There are two ways to wait for the pool:

- `stop()` requests an immediate stop. Running jobs are allowed to finish, but queued jobs are not processed.
- `join()` waits until the queue is empty and all jobs have finished, then stops the pool.

For a clean shutdown after all queued jobs have been processed, call `join()`:

```spice
import "std/os/thread-pool";

f<int> main() {
    ThreadPool pool = ThreadPool(4s);

    // Enqueue 10 jobs
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        int index = i;
        pool.enqueue(p() {
            printf("Running job %d\n", index);
        });
    }

    pool.start();

    // Wait until all jobs are done, then stop the pool
    pool.join();
}
```

### Pausing and resuming

`pause()` tells the worker threads to finish their current job and then wait. While paused, queued jobs are not picked up. Use `resume()` to let the worker threads continue. The helper `isPaused()` reports the current pause state:

```spice
import "std/os/thread-pool";

f<int> main() {
    ThreadPool pool = ThreadPool(2s);

    pool.enqueue(p() {
        // Do something
    });

    pool.start();
    pool.pause();

    // New jobs stay queued while the pool is paused
    pool.enqueue(p() {
        // Do something else
    });

    pool.resume();
    pool.join();
}
```

### Complete example

A full example that enqueues several jobs, runs them in parallel, and waits for them to finish:

```spice
import "std/os/thread-pool";

f<int> main() {
    ThreadPool pool = ThreadPool(4s);

    // Enqueue jobs
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
        int index = i;
        pool.enqueue(p() {
            printf("Job %d finished\n", index);
        });
    }

    printf("Queued: %d, Workers: %d\n", pool.getQueuedJobCount(), pool.getWorkerThreadCount());

    // Run them in parallel
    pool.start();
    pool.join();

    printf("All jobs are done.\n");
}
```

### Querying pool state

- `getQueuedJobCount()` returns the number of jobs waiting to be processed.
- `getRunningJobCount()` returns the number of jobs currently running.
- `getWorkerThreadCount()` returns the number of worker threads.
- `isPaused()` returns whether the pool is paused.

